{
  "summary": "The previous proposal has several significant gaps. First, the naming is confusing: it proposes adding 'awaiting-human-approval' as a new phase when 'final-human-approval' already exists for post-implementation gating. Two similarly-named human-hold phases will confuse both users and pipeline logic — a clearer distinction like 'human-gate-plan' vs 'final-human-approval' would be needed, or better yet, reuse the existing phase with metadata to distinguish pre-impl vs post-impl holds. Second, the proposal's insertion point is inconsistent with the actual code: it says to check the flag 'after plan review PASS' and route to the hold phase 'instead of approved,' but the original feature description says items should not go 'from approved to implementation automatically.' These are different pipeline positions — the proposal needs to clearly define whether the gate is pre-approved (blocking plan acceptance) or post-approved (blocking the approved→spec-writing transition). The actual codebase shows plan review PASS leads to further pipeline stages, so the check placement matters enormously. Third, the board-level default mechanism lacks lifecycle semantics: if a board flips 'human_gate_before_impl' to true mid-pipeline, do items already past the gate point retroactively get pulled back? The proposal is silent on this. Fourth, there's no consideration of the WebUI/TUI visibility — users need to see at a glance WHY an item is in a hold phase (human-gated by policy vs. human-gated by review failure vs. human-gated by max retries), but the proposal treats all holds identically. Finally, the proposal doesn't address idempotency: if the pipeline runner restarts and re-processes an item already in the human-approval hold, the flag check needs to be stage-aware to avoid re-routing items that a human already released."
}
